     The DDBB__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() macro adds _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___n_a_m_e to the list of top-level com‐
     mands.  Invoking _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___n_a_m_e from ddb will call _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___f_u_n_c_t_i_o_n.

     The DDBB__SSHHOOWW__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() and DDBB__SSHHOOWW__AALLLL__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() macros are roughly equiv‐
     alent to DDBB__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() but in these cases, _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___n_a_m_e is a sub-command
     of the ddb sshhooww command and sshhooww aallll command, respectively.

     The DDBB__TTAABBLLEE__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() macro is also similar to DDBB__CCOOMMMMAANNDD() but adds the
     new command as a sub-command of the ddb command _t_a_b_l_e.

     The DDBB__AALLIIAASS(), DDBB__SSHHOOWW__AALLIIAASS(), DDBB__SSHHOOWW__AALLLL__AALLIIAASS(), and
     DDBB__TTAABBLLEE__AALLIIAASS() macros register the existing _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___f_u_n_c_t_i_o_n under the
     alternative command name _a_l_i_a_s___n_a_m_e.

     The _FLAGS variants of these commands allow the programmer to specify a
     value for the _f_l_a_g field of the command structure.  The possible flag
     values are defined alongside _s_t_r_u_c_t _d_b___c_o_m_m_a_n_d in <_d_d_b_/_d_d_b_._h>.

     The general command syntax: ccoommmmaanndd[/_m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r] _a_d_d_r_e_s_s[,_c_o_u_n_t], trans‐
     lates into the following parameters for _c_o_m_m_a_n_d___f_u_n_c_t_i_o_n:

           _a_d_d_r          The address passed to the command as an argument.

           _h_a_v_e___a_d_d_r     A boolean value that is true if the addr field is
                         valid.

           _c_o_u_n_t         The number of quad words starting at offset _a_d_d_r that
                         the command must process.

           _m_o_d_i_f         A pointer to the string of modifiers.  That is, a se‐
                         ries of symbols used to pass some options to the com‐
                         mand.  For example, the eexxaammiinnee command will display
                         words in decimal form if it is passed the modifier
                         "d".

     The DDBB__DDEEFFIINNEE__TTAABBLLEE() macro adds a new command _n_a_m_e as a sub-command of
     the existing command table _p_a_r_e_n_t.  The new command defines a table named
     _t_a_b_l_e which contains sub-commands.  New commands and aliases can be added
     to this table by passing _t_a_b_l_e as the first argument to one of the DB_TA‐
     BLE_ macros.

